        "content": "<p>Gelbpunkt opened <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809\">PR #14809</a> from <code>Gelbpunkt:s390x-128bit-atomic-rmw</code> to <code>bytecodealliance:main</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Unfortunately, it is currently not possible to implement this in ISLE. I tried, and failed miserably. The implementation for 64-bit can be done in ISLE because it can hardcode two physical GPRs - r0 and r1 - which are excluded from regalloc. This is not the case with the VRs, which we are using for the 128-bit integers, so we end up violating SSA rules if we attempt to implement this in ISLE (previous attempt: <a href=\"https://github.com/Gelbpunkt/wasmtime/commit/124d0212ff470557cf5b6fc2aa107daa1d878edc\">https://github.com/Gelbpunkt/wasmtime/commit/124d0212ff470557cf5b6fc2aa107daa1d878edc</a>).</p>\n<p>On a technical level: We use a CAS loop for this, just like with the 64-bit atomics. It is actually quite straightforward to implement, however there's a small caveat: We cannot use the vector min/max operations prior to z17, so we need to implement a fallback for the baseline if VXRS_EXT3 is not available.</p>\n<p>I've tested this in qemu-user with and without VXRS_EXT3, and the runtest passes in both environments.</p>\n<p>cc @theotherjimmy @uweigand </p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 633029044,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792421204
    },
