    {
        "content": "<p>Gelbpunkt edited a <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809#issuecomment-6271200220\">comment</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809\">PR #14809</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><del>Runtests for s390x in CI are throwing SIGILL in qemu, that's weird. They pass for me locally in qemu-user and also natively on z15. Is the QEMU in CI just too old or what's going on here? <span aria-label=\"thinking\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f914\" role=\"img\" title=\"thinking\">:thinking:</span></del></p>\n<p>Didn't happen on the re-run, but either way probably a QEMU issue</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 633105188,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792436795
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>bjorn3 <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809#issuecomment-6293156797\">commented</a> on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809\">PR #14809</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I've tested this with the rust libcore test suite as well as the portable-atomic test suite with <code>CARGO_CFG_SANITIZE=thread</code> to make it the atomic intrinsics rather than inline asm.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 633336354,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792506310
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"memo\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4dd\" role=\"img\" title=\"memo\">:memo:</span> uweigand submitted <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809#pullrequestreview-5586388466\">PR review</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Unfortunately, it is currently not possible to implement this in ISLE. I tried, and failed miserably. The implementation for 64-bit can be done in ISLE because it can hardcode two physical GPRs - r0 and r1 - which are excluded from regalloc. This is not the case with the VRs, which we are using for the 128-bit integers, so we end up violating SSA rules if we attempt to implement this in ISLE (previous attempt: <a href=\"https://github.com/Gelbpunkt/wasmtime/commit/124d0212ff470557cf5b6fc2aa107daa1d878edc\">https://github.com/Gelbpunkt/wasmtime/commit/124d0212ff470557cf5b6fc2aa107daa1d878edc</a>).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think it might be possible to extend the <code>Loop</code> construct with a list of temporary registers that are allocated as fixed early-def, and then use registers from that list as scratch registers inside the loop.</p>\n<p>However, I'm not sure it's really worth the effort at this point.  I added <code>Loop</code> primarily to allow more concise encoding of the various sub-word CAS loops.  For the i128 case, implementing it in the emitter seems straightforward enough.</p>\n<p>Generally, this implementation looks good to me, but I think we could be more frugal with scratch register use, see inline comments.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 633337894,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792506581
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"speech balloon\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4ac\" role=\"img\" title=\"speech balloon\">:speech_balloon:</span> uweigand created <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809#discussion_r4326057749\">PR review comment</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This is really unfortunate.   If we don't need <code>scratch1</code> and <code>scratch2</code>, will this already fix this?   The <code>scratch3</code> and <code>scratch4</code> must be hard-coded anyway, so maybe we don't need them in the insn either ...</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 633337900,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792506582
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"speech balloon\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4ac\" role=\"img\" title=\"speech balloon\">:speech_balloon:</span> uweigand created <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14809#discussion_r4326059209\">PR review comment</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I don't think we need <code>scratch1</code> or <code>scratch2</code> here.  It should be possible to only use <code>rd</code> to hold any scratch VR values, something along the lines of:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Rust\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">rd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mem</span>\n<span class=\"n\">scratch3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rd</span>\n\n<span class=\"k\">loop</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"nc\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">XChg</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">scratch4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rn</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">arith</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">rd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Op</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rd</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rn</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">scratch4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rd</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">case</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">min</span><span class=\"o\">/</span><span class=\"n\">max</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">fallback</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"nc\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">VecInt128</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">US</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"n\">CmpHi</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tmp</span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nc\">rd</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">rd</span><span class=\"o\">|</span><span class=\"n\">rn</span><span class=\"p\">],</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">rn</span><span class=\"o\">|</span><span class=\"n\">rd</span><span class=\"p\">]))</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">scratch4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">rn</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">else</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"n\">scratch4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">scratch3</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">scratch3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">AtomicCas128</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">scratch3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">scratch4</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">&lt;-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">scratch3</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">if</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cas</span><span class=\"o\">-</span><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">goto</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">loop</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Multiple assignments to <code>rd</code> should be fine as it is marked as early-def (and they happen with your version already anyway).</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 633337902,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1792506582
    },
